Commit: pending
Date: 2026-08-02
Type: Fix -- one classification rule instead of two
Request-or-TODO: PIPE-042, PIPE-006a
Outcome: pipeline.ClassifyDecline becomes the single implementation of the
  skip-audit classification, called by the coordinator when it writes the audit
  and by the browser when it recomputes from the wire.
  Two copies existed for a few hours and disagreed immediately. The coordinator
  classified a stage with no declared check as unimplemented; the browser folded
  that case in with may-not-satisfy and answered no-check-by-design, or
  principled-decline when the stage appeared in Section33Resolved. The same
  ledger therefore classified differently depending on who read it -- and in the
  direction that flatters, since a stage the flow gained without a performer
  would have been shown to a reader as a deliberate design decision
Affected-behavior: The browser and the coordinator now agree. The coordinator's
  own answers are unchanged; the browser's change on the unbound case
Compatibility-or-migration: None
Verification: The unbound case is pinned by a test that names why it matters,
  and a second test separates a §33-resolved decline, a by-design stage and an
  unimplemented one
Known-limitations: The classification is still not on the wire, so the browser
  recomputes it from shared static tables rather than reading it. That is why
  two implementations existed at all, and carrying it on the wire would remove
  the need entirely
