Change-ID: CL-20260802-028
Commit: 49061c4
Date: 2026-08-02
Type: The engine ladder grows to 250 rungs and is judged by properties of the
  work rather than by paths it was told to write to
Request-or-TODO: User: "expand the list to 150 programs, make sure each new
  program is more complex than the last, above 100 lets start making it
  integrate with the golang http framework"; then "the next 50 are full web
  server multi stage requirements, make them complex like how a production
  software company, many acs to hit, complex, large and fine grained";
  "include sql(ite) related acs aswell"; "I dont like that you have filenames in
  the test instructions, that should be abstracted away into the graph and it
  and we should let the agent determine even the file names and func groupings";
  "the complexity comes from the integration of 3rd party impure libraries for
  side effect heavy code, lets make sure our structure can work well with less
  structured code in a sane and scalable way"; "make sure these test if ran
  store the pipeline artifacts ... lets add a flag to toggle full pipeline or a
  passes over each test without artifacts"
Outcome: The ladder was 48 single programs. It is now 250 in five bands: harder
  algorithms and multi-package systems (49-100), HTTP services that serve and
  call themselves on an ephemeral port (101-150), production specifications with
  interacting acceptance criteria over a real SQLite database (151-200), and the
  same work built on 29 third-party libraries (201-250). No requirement names a
  file, a package or a function: the layout is the run's to decide and part of
  what is judged. The checks were rebuilt to match. They read the produced work
  once with go/parser and ask about properties -- how many packages the work was
  separated into, whether the package an idea landed in reaches outside itself,
  how far a library was allowed to spread, whether an interface exists to
  substitute at, whether two runs print the same bytes, whether anything was
  left on disk -- rather than about paths. Containment is what replaces purity
  for impure code: a library may be reachable from one package, and asking a
  package that drives a database driver to be pure describes nothing. The suite
  also gained two modes. Isolated is the default and gives every rung its own
  database; shared runs the whole ladder against one project so the recall stage
  has a history to search, which is the only way that stage has ever had
  anything to find.
Affected-behavior: Test-only. No product code changed. Three defects that only
  the shared mode exposes were fixed in the fixture: carryOut used constant
  idempotency keys, so a second rung sharing a database would have matched the
  first rung's key and been handed the first rung's task; the completion wait
  read the thread's narration, which already holds the previous rung's
  "Finished:" and would have returned at once; and four pre-existing rungs
  passed command-line arguments containing spaces, which the acceptance block
  joins and the engine's parser splits, so those rungs showed the model a
  different invocation from the one they were judged by. Their inputs moved to
  standard input, which the format escapes correctly.
  A fourth defect was fixed on the user's instruction: the ladder ran whenever a
  provider key could be found, which meant any `go test ./...` -- including the
  one the pre-push hook runs -- charged 250 live model runs to whoever had a key
  in their .env. It now skips unless CODEFLUX_LADDER names a mode, so spending
  money is something a person asks for rather than something a hook does.
Compatibility-or-migration: Running the ladder now requires CODEFLUX_LADDER to
  be set to isolated or shared; without it the test skips. The rung table moved
  out of the test function into ladderRungs so the checks that need no provider
  key can read it.
Verification: gofmt clean; go vet ./internal/coordinator exits 0; the fourteen
  provider-free tests in engine_produced_work_test.go pass in 0.35s. The 250
  rungs themselves have not been run against a model: that needs a key and
  spends real money, and the expected transcripts are reasoned rather than
  observed.
Dev-Log: DL-20260802-038

Change-ID: CL-20260802-027
Commit: 287fc7b
Date: 2026-08-02
Type: A push carrying Go changes is refused below 80% statement coverage
Request-or-TODO: User: "refine repo rules lets aim for 80% code coverage to push
  to dev"
Outcome: .githooks/pre-push runs the suite with a coverage profile, reads the
  total through go tool cover -func, and refuses the push below 80%. The floor
  is checked on push rather than on commit because coverage describes the branch
  being published, not every step along the way. A push with no Go change skips
  the measurement entirely. A failing suite is refused rather than measured: a
  percentage from a partial run describes only the packages that passed, and
  reads as reassuring while meaning nothing. CODEFLUX_MIN_COVERAGE overrides the
  floor for bisecting and for establishing a baseline. The rule is written into
  AGENTS.md and CONTRIBUTING, both of which state that coverage is a floor and
  not a target -- statements executed is not behaviour verified, which is what
  the mutation-score gate is for
Affected-behavior: Pushing Go changes now runs the suite locally first
Compatibility-or-migration: The hook only applies to clones that have run
  git config core.hooksPath .githooks
Verification: The hook parses under sh -n; the skip path was exercised against a
  real ref pair; Go-file detection was verified against a range that contains Go
  changes and one that does not; the total parser was run against a real profile
  and the tenths comparison confirmed to block at 26.9 against 80
