Outcome: AGENTS.md gains "Frontend changes must be driven in a browser", which
  replaces a single soft bullet about keyboard and accessibility checks. A Go
  test over a pure projection no longer counts as verification of a frontend
  change: the control a person would use must be operated through the mounted
  Playwright harness, and the check must assert the event, the resulting state,
  and the render, because a handler that fires without changing state, state
  that changes without re-rendering, and a render on the wrong boundary are
  three different defects with one symptom. The keyboard path is walked in full
  including focus restoration after an overlay closes; the loading, empty,
  error, and truncated states are covered rather than only the state the work
  was built in; and a screenshot must be taken AND opened, judged for legibility
  at rendered scale, overlap, clipping, spacing, and focus ring containment. The
  section closes the three false claims: a projection test, an unopened
  screenshot, and a successful build are none of them evidence that an interface
  works. docs/developing.md's frontend golden path was rewritten to match and
  points at AGENTS.md as authoritative
Affected-behavior: None at runtime. This governs how frontend work is verified
Compatibility-or-migration: None. Existing mounted checks already satisfy the
  rule; it is newly stated, not newly invented
Verification: No Go source touched, so the gates are unaffected. The harness
  capabilities cited -- Click, Fill, Press, Keyboard().Press, Focus,
  SelectOption, Screenshot, SetViewportSize, the routes/states/viewports
  cross-product, and the 15s-120s timeouts -- were each read out of
  internal/frontendtest rather than assumed
