Date: 2026-08-01
Type: Reachable plan approval, so a typed request reaches a running worker
Request-or-TODO: User: "does it look like the app is working to you????? e2e
  test it ffs"
Outcome: A request typed into the browser now creates a task, records the
  approval of its forecast and budget, binds what was reviewed, starts it, and
  spawns a worker against a real Git worktree
Affected-behavior: Sending a request recorded a message and a bare draft task
  with no policy, forecast, or budget, and stopped there. Of the five steps
  between a request and a worker, three could not be reached by any client:
  CreateTask and StartTask existed as RPCs the interface never called, and plan
  approval and preflight binding had no RPC at all, so a created task could
  never leave Draft. Every test passed while this was true, because the suite
  proved the page mounts, connects, and does not lie, and never proved the
  product does its job. TaskService gains ApprovePlan, which the plan catalog
  had declared and nothing had implemented; it walks a task through
  Forecasting and AwaitingPlanApproval to Ready with the approval recorded on
  the transition that means "a person said run this", then binds the exact
  policy and forecast that were reviewed and returns the binding for StartTask
  to name back. Intake's four environment requirements — base revision,
  toolchain, validation profile, baseline model — are now observed by the
  coordinator instead of demanded from the caller, because they are facts
  about the machine rather than decisions and a browser supplying them would
  have been guessing. The kind of change is the one thing nothing can observe,
  so the composer asks for it and refuses to start a task without it. Two
  failures this exposed are also fixed: Git's own explanation of a failed
  command was discarded, so every worktree failure read "exit status 128" —
  the actual message was "Filename too long", Windows refusing a path past 260
  characters, which left runs recorded as started with no worktree and no
  worker; and the workspace read "No task yet" against a live worker because
  the session snapshot it drew from was fetched before the task existed and
  nothing refetched it
Compatibility-or-migration: One added RPC, TaskService.ApprovePlan, already
  present in the backend function catalog. CreateTaskRequest's environment
  fields become optional and are filled by the coordinator when empty; a caller
  that names them keeps them. SendMessage no longer asks for a draft task.
  storage gains GetCurrentTaskForecast; gitwork worktree creation sets
  core.longpaths and surfaces Git's stderr in its errors
Verification: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...`, and `codeflux-dev lint` pass.
  New coordinator tests drive intake, approval, and start against a booted
  application and assert a worktree exists. A new end-to-end test types a
  request into a real browser, declares a kind of change, sends it, and fails
  unless the database shows a running task with a worktree holding a checkout
  and the workspace stops reporting that nothing is running
